</style></head><body><main class="shell"><header class="top"><div class="brand">I Love My Body<small>NILESH YADAV · PRIVATE PATIENT JOURNEY</small></div><div class="tools"><button class="btn" onclick="window.print()">Print / PDF</button><a class="btn" href="?logout=1">Log out</a></div></header><section class="hero"><div class="ey">START WITH THE TREATMENT STORY</div><h1>What happened, what the medicines do, and what ILMB is trying to restore.</h1><p>This dashboard follows one language throughout: <b>finding → body effect → medicine effect → collateral burden → ILMB same-goal target → measurement.</b> The medicine comparison is the centre of the page.</p><div class="quick"><div class="q"><span>Index event</span><b>23 Aug 2024</b><span>Right-MCA ischemic stroke</span></div><div class="q"><span>Later clinical state</span><b>No recorded deficit</b><span>26 Jul 2025</span></div><div class="q"><span>Key metabolic signal</span><b>TG 301</b><span>5 May 2026</span></div><div class="q"><span>Key unresolved question</span><b>Current MCA flow</b><span>Perfusion/collaterals not quantified</span></div></div></section><nav class="nav"><button class="active" data-panel="medicines">1. Medicines + ILMB</button><button data-panel="reports">2. My reports</button><button data-panel="plan">3. ILMB plan</button><button data-panel="numbers">4. What we measure</button><button data-panel="science">5. Background science</button></nav>
<section id="medicines" class="panel active"><div class="section"><div class="ey">THE COMPARATIVE CHART</div><h2>Every medicine against the same body goal</h2><p class="lead">This is the chart to read first. A side effect is a known possibility, not something assumed to be happening to Nilesh.</p><div class="compare-wrap"><table class="compare"><thead><tr><th>Medicine</th><th>Desired effect</th><th>How it achieves it</th><th>Important collateral / side-effect path</th><th>ILMB target</th><th>What proves the result</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Clopidogrel 75 mg<small>Clopilet</small></td><td>Platelet aggregation ↓</td><td>P2Y12 inhibition → ADP signalling ↓</td><td class="harm">The same platelet inhibition that reduces thrombosis also reduces normal hemostasis, so bleeding/bruising can increase; rare TTP is also reported.</td><td class="ilmb">Reduce pathological thrombosis through endothelium, metabolic load, fibrinolysis and vascular state <b>without deliberately disabling P2Y12</b>, if equivalent protection can be demonstrated.</td><td>Recurrence/bleeding context; CBC/platelets; platelet-function testing only if specifically indicated.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Aspirin 150 mg<small>Ecosprin</small></td><td>TXA₂-mediated platelet aggregation ↓</td><td>COX-1 inhibition → thromboxane ↓</td><td class="harm">Platelet hemostasis ↓; aspirin also carries GI bleeding/irritation risk.</td><td class="ilmb">Reduce net thrombosis through alternate vascular/endothelial/fibrinolytic paths while avoiding unnecessary GI/bleeding burden.</td><td>Bleeding/GI symptoms, CBC/platelets where relevant, recurrence and vascular state.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Amlodipine 5 mg<small>Amlodac AT component</small></td><td>BP / vascular resistance ↓</td><td>L-type Ca²⁺ blockade → arterial relaxation</td><td class="harm">Vasodilation can also cause edema, dizziness and hypotension; edema is a common dose-related adverse effect.</td><td class="ilmb">Lower vascular resistance/BP through physiologic endothelial/autonomic conditioning without pharmacologic Ca-channel blockade.</td><td>SBP/DBP, orthostatic response if needed, edema and dizziness.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Atenolol 50 mg<small>Amlodac AT component</small></td><td>HR and cardiac workload ↓</td><td>β1 blockade</td><td class="harm">Can cause bradycardia, hypotension, fatigue, tiredness, dizziness and suppress exercise HR response.</td><td class="ilmb">Lower resting cardiac demand through improved conditioning/autonomic regulation without blocking β1.</td><td>Resting HR/BP, fatigue/dizziness, workload and perceived exertion.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Atorvastatin 40 mg<small>Lipvas 40</small></td><td>LDL/apoB ↓</td><td>HMG-CoA reductase inhibition → LDL clearance ↑</td><td class="harm">Can cause muscle injury/myopathy and liver-enzyme abnormalities; rare rhabdomyolysis is documented.</td><td class="ilmb">Lower LDL/apoB through intestinal, hepatic-substrate and metabolic routes without directly inhibiting HMG-CoA reductase.</td><td>LDL/non-HDL/apoB; ALT/AST; muscle symptoms, CK when clinically relevant.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fenofibrate 160 mg<small>Finobrate 160</small></td><td>TG-rich particles ↓</td><td>PPARα activation → TG clearance ↑</td><td class="harm">Can cause liver injury/enzyme rise, muscle injury, reversible creatinine rise and gallstones; muscle risk is higher when combined with a statin.</td><td class="ilmb">Reduce triglyceride production and increase triglyceride/fatty-acid utilisation through food chemistry, hepatic substrate control, gut-metabolic routes and muscle activity — without pharmacologic PPARα activation.</td><td>Fasting triglycerides, ALT/AST, creatinine/eGFR and muscle symptoms.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cod liver oil 300 mg<small>Seacap</small></td><td>Nutrient exposure</td><td>Depends on exact EPA/DHA/vitamin A/vitamin D content</td><td class="harm">Dose-dependent nutrient excess and interaction burden are possible; bleeding relevance depends on the actual EPA/DHA exposure.</td><td class="ilmb">Read the exact label chemistry → quantify chemical input → compare with measured requirement → track response.</td><td>Exact label composition plus relevant nutrient biomarkers.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Neurobion Forte</td><td>B-vitamin cofactor support</td><td>Provides B-vitamin cofactors used in multiple biochemical pathways</td><td class="harm">Unnecessary or excessive dosing can also create effects; exact formulation matters.</td><td class="ilmb">Requirement-first food/supplement calculation for each nutrient instead of treating a multivitamin as one undifferentiated intervention.</td><td>B12 and need-specific markers where relevant.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div><div class="note"><b>Critical boundary:</b> ILMB can work on the net thrombosis state, BP, lipid burden, metabolic state and rehabilitation targets. It does not claim that food or exercise directly replaces P2Y12 or COX-1/TXA₂ inhibition unless equivalent protection is actually demonstrated.</div></div></section>
<section id="reports" class="panel"><div class="section"><div class="ey">MY REPORTS</div><h2>Read the story from the source documents</h2><div class="timeline"><div class="event"><div><b>23 Aug 2024</b><p>MRI brain</p></div><div><strong>Finding</strong><p>Multiple fresh non-haemorrhagic right fronto-parieto-temporal infarcts.</p></div><div><strong>Meaning</strong><p>Several right-brain regions were injured because their blood supply failed.</p></div></div><div class="event"><div><b>23 Aug 2024</b><p>MRA brain</p></div><div><strong>Finding</strong><p>High-grade right MCA occlusion from origin with severe paucity of distal branches.</p></div><div><strong>Meaning</strong><p>The main arterial route to a large right-brain territory was severely compromised.</p></div></div><div class="event"><div><b>21 Jul 2025</b><p>MRI/MRA follow-up</p></div><div><strong>Finding</strong><p>Right MCA M1 still showed no flow; faint M2 enhancement questioned as collateral.</p></div><div><strong>Meaning</strong><p>Severe vessel abnormality persisted. Alternative collateral supply may exist but was not quantified.</p></div></div><div class="event"><div><b>26 Jul 2025</b><p>Clinical follow-up</p></div><div><strong>Finding</strong><p>Normal speech; no recorded deficit.</p></div><div><strong>Meaning</strong><p>Function recovered much more than the vessel image alone suggests. Collateral flow and neuroplasticity therefore matter.</p></div></div><div class="event"><div><b>31 Jul 2026</b><p>Plain CT brain</p></div><div><strong>Finding</strong><p>Old right-sided infarcts; no acute infarct, haemorrhage or mass.</p></div><div><strong>Meaning</strong><p>Reassuring for no new acute lesion. It does not answer current MCA lumen, collateral reserve or tissue perfusion.</p></div></div></div></div></section>
<section id="plan" class="panel"><div class="section"><div class="ey">MY ILMB TREATMENT</div><h2>Step-by-step method</h2><div class="steps"><div class="step"><b>1 · Protect established benefit</b><p>Keep current prescribed treatment unchanged while ILMB measures the body state and response.</p></div><div class="step"><b>2 · Establish current vascular state</b><p>Answer current right-MCA lumen/flow, collaterals and regional perfusion; document the present indication/duration for aspirin + clopidogrel.</p></div><div class="step"><b>3 · Repeat unresolved measurements</b><p>Fasting lipids, glucose, CBC/platelets, liver enzymes, creatinine/eGFR, potassium, TSH/free T4, 25-OH-D, urinalysis/ACR.</p></div><div class="step"><b>4 · Quantify function</b><p>Bilateral grip, arm/leg strength, 10-m walk, sit-to-stand, dexterity, balance, ROM and tone.</p></div><div class="step"><b>5 · Capture real input chemistry</b><p>Three normal weighed days of food, drinks and supplements. Grams → composition → chemical identity → pathway.</p></div><div class="step"><b>6 · Select only relevant ILMB modules</b><p>Food chemistry, movement dose, rehabilitation, sleep/autonomic and other modalities are activated only for measured abnormal nodes.</p></div><div class="step"><b>7 · Hold inputs steady</b><p>A defined intervention must be delivered consistently long enough to measure a response.</p></div><div class="step"><b>8 · Re-measure Nilesh's response</b><p>Calculate change in the actual output rather than assuming a population-average effect.</p></div></div></div></section>
<section id="numbers" class="panel"><div class="section"><div class="ey">WHAT WE MEASURE</div><h2>Known values and unresolved outputs</h2><div class="metrics"><div class="metric warn"><small>Triglycerides</small><strong>301 mg/dL</strong><small>LOINC 2571-8</small></div><div class="metric warn"><small>ALT</small><strong>81 U/L</strong><small>LOINC 1742-6</small></div><div class="metric"><small>Creatinine</small><strong>1.27 mg/dL</strong><small>LOINC 2160-0</small></div><div class="metric"><small>eGFR</small><strong>72</strong><small>Kidney function context</small></div><div class="metric warn"><small>Potassium</small><strong>5.3 mmol/L</strong><small>LOINC 2823-3</small></div><div class="metric warn"><small>Platelets</small><strong>133 ×10³/µL</strong><small>LOINC 777-3</small></div><div class="metric risk"><small>25-OH Vitamin D</small><strong>13.9 ng/mL</strong><small>LOINC 62292-8</small></div><div class="metric warn"><small>TSH</small><strong>5.386</strong><small>LOINC 3016-3</small></div><div class="metric"><small>HbA1c</small><strong>5.8%</strong><small>LOINC 4548-4</small></div></div><div class="note"><b>Still missing:</b> current right-MCA perfusion/collaterals, quantitative motor baseline, current repeat fasting labs and exact cod-liver-oil chemistry.</div></div></section>
<section id="science" class="panel"><div class="section"><div class="ey">BACKGROUND ONLY</div><h2>LOINC, ChEBI and Reactome stay behind the patient story</h2><div class="flow"><span class="node">Patient result</span><span class="arr">→</span><span class="node">LOINC exact test identity</span><span class="arr">→</span><span class="node">Body state</span><span class="arr">→</span><span class="node">ChEBI chemical identity</span><span class="arr">→</span><span class="node">Reactome pathway</span><span class="arr">→</span><span class="node">ILMB intervention</span><span class="arr">→</span><span class="node">Re-measure</span></div><div class="identity"><span>LOINC 2571-8 · triglycerides</span><span>LOINC 4548-4 · HbA1c</span><span>LOINC 1742-6 · ALT</span><span>LOINC 777-3 · platelets</span><span>ChEBI 16480 · nitric oxide</span><span>ChEBI 15843 · arachidonic acid</span><span>ChEBI 17968 · butyrate</span><span>ChEBI 16828 · L-tryptophan</span></div><div class="note"><b>Rule:</b> unknown is never converted to zero, and an unverified mapping is never allowed to masquerade as an exact treatment equation.</div></div></section>
</main><script>const buttons=[...document.querySelectorAll('[data-panel]')],panels=[...document.querySelectorAll('.panel')];function show(id){buttons.forEach(b=>b.classList.toggle('active',b.dataset.panel===id));panels.forEach(p=>p.classList.toggle('active',p.id===id));history.replaceState(null,'','#'+id);scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})}buttons.forEach(b=>b.onclick=()=>show(b.dataset.panel));const h=location.hash.slice(1);if(document.getElementById(h))show(h);</script></body></html>